r and  we're just left with X plus 2 times 1  which is X plus 2 ok - when we  distribute this fraction to this second  fraction here you can see the X plus  twos cancel see numerator and  denominator and we're just left with X  plus h plus 2 now notice I kept that in  parentheses that's important and then in  the denominator we just have h  times this whole quantity x plus h plus  2 times X plus 2 ok so now we're going  to do is we're going to simplify here  I'm going to distribute the negative  okay into the parenthesis so that's  going to give us let's see negative X  minus H minus 2 so that X's are going to  cancel  the twos are gonna cancel because once  positive one is negative and we're left  with negative H in the numerator and you  can see that these H's are gonna cancel  ok so now we're left with is just  negative 1 in the numerator this is your  difference quotient if you're just  simplifying you can leave it that at  that stage right there but if you want  to take it one step further to find the  derivative we would then take the limit  as H approaches zero if we put 0 in for  each that's going to go to zero and you  can see we have X plus 2 times X plus 2  which is negative 1 over X plus 2  squared and that's going to be your  derivative so depending on where you're  at you know with learning about the  difference quotient whether it's finding  the derivative or just stopping at you  know simplifying that difference  quotient you can do it either way okay  number two we've got f of X equals  square root of x minus 3 so same process  working with the difference quotient  whatever is in the parentheses goes in  for X on the right okay so what we have  is we have X plus h now minus 3 minus f  of X so f of X is our original function  here so square root of x minus 3 all  divided by each ok so now what we want  to do is we want to rationalize the  numerator and what we're going to do is  we're going to multiply by the conjugate  so if this was a minus here we're gonna  multiply by the same quantity in the  numerator X plus h minus 3 plus now  square root of x minus 3 whatever you do  to the numerator you want to do the same  thing to the denominator okay if this  was a plus then I would make these -  okay so it's the opposite sign in  between so now we're going to do is  we're going to multiply these together  these two binomials and so you can see  whenever you multiply a square root  times itself you just get the quantity  underneath that square root okay and  then here what happens is the  enter two quantities and the outer two  quantities they're gonna cancel because  see how one is negative and one is  positive so they're gonna cancel each  other out and then the last term times  the last term we've got a square root  times the square root which again  whatever is underneath is what you're  gonna be ending up with and negative  times a positive is a negative  okay so put that in parenthesis all  divided by each times this denominator  so that's X plus h minus three plus  square root of x minus three okay so now  let's see if we distribute this negative  in here we get negative x plus three and  you can see that X's are cancelling the  negative three in the positive three are  cancelling and we're just left with each  in the numerator but notice how that H  and the numerator is going to cancel  with the H and the denominator and we're  just left with one over square root of x  plus h minus three plus square root of x  minus three now you can stop there if  you're just working with the difference  quotient but if you're trying to find  the derivative you want to take that  limit as H approaches zero if I put zero  in for each that's going to cancel that  out and you can see we have square root  of x minus three plus another square  root of x minus 3 which is 1 over 2  times the square root of x minus 3 and  that's your derivative if you want to  take it further you can rationalize by  multiplying the numerator and  denominator by square square root of x  minus 3 but we'll just leave it at that  for right now okay in our third example  f of x equals x squared minus 2x we're  gonna do that same process whatever's in  the parenthesis is going to go in for X  on the right here we've got two x's on  the right so we have to put it in for  both of these X's we're going to get X  plus h squared minus 2 times X plus h  minus f of X now f of X is this original  equation here make sure you put it in  parenthesis okay so you take it as a  group all divided by H okay so now we  want to do is you don't want to just  square both of these terms here you want  to oil this out so X plus h squared is  really like X plus h times another X  plus h right because squared you've got  two of them and then distribute the  negative two negative two X minus 2h  distribute the negative here negative x  squared plus 2x  / H okay now if we multiply this out  we're gonna get x squared plus 2 HX plus  h squared so notice that HX on the  inside XH on the outside gives us the  middle term - H X okay so now you can  see we've got a negative x squared and  positive x squared those cancel so  you've gotta see what else do we have  here we've got C the 2x and the negative  2x which are cancelling and we're left  with two H X plus h squared - 2 H all  divided by H now notice there's an H and  each one of these terms in the numerator  if we factor it out we get 2x plus h  minus 2 all divided by H now I can see  those HS are cancelling and this is your  difference quotient 2x plus h minus 2  you can leave it like that but if you  want to take it one step further to find  the derivative we take the limit as H  approaches zero so this is going to go  to zero and so now we just have to X  minus 2 and that's your derivative which  remember is a formula for finding the  slope at any point along the curve so if  you want to see more examples with the  difference quotient follow me over to  that video right there  and now we'll do some more examples I'll  see you in the next video

